#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Scrub a captured payload for a public corpus, against a manifest.

The corpus is public and captures come from real machines. The previous
scrubber guessed a value's class from its shape, and shape-guessing is what
rewrote every disk's phys_path into the documentation range, left real WWNs
untouched because devid spells them without 0x, and walked past JSON integers
entirely so real pool GUIDs went out verbatim (DESIGN 21). The collector
already knows what each field is — so the classification now travels with the
collector, as a manifest, and this tool guesses nothing:

  harness/scrub/<collector>.json maps payload field paths to
      {"discloses": nothing|identity|location|content|secret,
       "format": wwn|guid_u64|serial|mac|ipv4|ipv6|hostname|name|path|prose
                 |unit_name|object_path,
       "payload": <payload stem this entry is scoped to, optional>}

  The last two are ENCODINGS rather than shapes: a producer that writes an
  identifier in its own escaping — systemd spells one partition UUID three
  ways in a single ListUnits row — hides it from every pattern below, so the
  pass runs over the folded reading and the escaping is restored afterwards.
  See ENCODED_FORMATS.

  paths are dotted; '*' matches one dict key or one list index; a trailing
  '~key' segment classifies the KEY strings of the dict it names (a dict of
  disks is keyed by the very WWNs its values carry). Every leaf must match
  exactly one entry or the run FAILS listing the unmatched paths —
  deny-by-default, because an unclassified field is a capture this tool does
  not understand, and the failure mode of passing it is publication.

  A dot INSIDE a segment is written with a leading backslash, because a
  filename is allowed one
  and the tree seam keys payloads by filename: `nix` captures a generation's
  optional `se-generation.json` beside its `nixos-version`, and without the
  escape that member is addressable by no pattern at all — which under
  deny-by-default means the whole payload is unscrubbable rather than
  silently passed.

  A key may be PREFIXED with a payload stem and a colon — "realpath:*.*" —
  which scopes the entry exactly as the "payload" member does. Both exist
  because the member cannot carry two entries for one pattern: a JSON object
  holds a key once, so a collector whose payloads share a shape can classify
  that shape for only one of them. `nix` is the first with four — realpath,
  readlink, mtime and exists are all one container and one member deep, and
  three of the four would have been unclassifiable. The prefix is recognised
  ONLY when the text before the first colon is the stem of a payload in this
  run, so a member that legitimately contains a colon (a PCI address, an
  object path) still reads as part of the pattern. Naming the payload both
  ways at once is refused rather than merged.

  One manifest covers every payload a collector captures, and those payloads
  do not all share a root. zpool's documents are keyed by fixed schema
  members (`pools`, `output_version`); the devlinks map's root keys ARE data,
  every one of them an alias path carrying a partition UUID. A '~key' entry
  at the root is right for the second and a misclassification of the first,
  and a path is all a pattern can say — so an entry may name the `payload`
  it applies to, by the same stem the replay seam and se-capture-guest use
  ("status", "list", "devlinks", "nft"). Unscoped entries apply to every
  payload, which is what nearly all of them want; a scoped and an unscoped
  entry matching the same leaf is the ordinary two-entries refusal, because
  which one governs must not depend on this file's iteration order.

  A payload is not always JSON: for some interfaces the native format IS a
  text document (os-release, the kernel hostname — DESIGN 20), and a text
  document has no leaves for a dotted path to name. Such a payload is
  classified WHOLE, by a payload-scoped entry whose classification sits
  under "whole" instead of at the top level:

      {"payload": "hostname", "whole": {"discloses": "location",
                                        "format": "hostname"}}

  A shaped format (hostname, mac, ipv4…) substitutes the document's one
  token with its surrounding whitespace preserved; no format (or name/path)
  runs the free-text pass over the whole document. A text payload with no
  whole entry is refused exactly as an unclassified leaf is — this tool
  used to crash on bytes it could not json.loads, and a crash is not a
  classification.

  "No leaves for a dotted path to name" is a property of the DOCUMENT, not
  of the file extension, so a .json payload that decodes to a bare scalar
  takes the same whole regime. A tree-shaped interface has one such payload
  per path it read, and `null` is how it records a path that was not there —
  which is a different answer from an empty document and must stay one.
  Before this, such a payload reached scrub_node's leaf branch at the root,
  where no pattern can match (every pattern has at least one segment), so
  the tool refused every capture of that shape with "(root): no manifest
  entry classifies this leaf" and no manifest could answer it.

  And `payload` may be a GLOB on a whole entry, because a transcription's
  payload set is one file per path read and those paths are the machine's,
  not the manifest author's. The classification is per FILE KIND — cpu.stat
  holds the kernel's CPU counters on every host that has one — so an entry
  says so once, and a stem matching no entry is refused exactly as before.
  Exact stems still work and still win by being the only match; two entries
  matching one payload is refused, so which one governs never depends on
  this file's iteration order.

  nothing   -> verbatim          identity/location/content -> substituted
  secret    -> the run is refused outright: a secret must never have been
               captured, and no amount of substitution repairs that

Substitution is KEYED: replacements derive from HMAC-SHA256(secret,
class:value), secret from SE_SCRUB_SECRET or a state file created on first
use. An unkeyed digest is a lookup table for anyone who can enumerate the
input space — a /10 of addresses falls in seconds. One original maps to one
replacement globally (the same WWN in three payloads is what teaches the join
those payloads exist to demonstrate), a replacement keeps its original's
shape and space, and a replacement is never itself scrubbed (parked out of
reach until the pass ends).

Replacements land where the independent detectors (se_harness/detectors.py)
expect anonymised material by convention — and by nothing else; the checker
shares no pattern, table or code path with this file:

  16-hex identifier bodies   first nibble 3 (NAA locally-assigned: no vendor)
  32-hex identifiers         prefix 5cb0
  guid_u64 of >= 15 digits   decimal band 1844… , always <= 2^64-1
  by-id serial tails         prefix ANON
  MAC                        02:… (unicast) / 03:… (a group address stays one)
  addresses                  own space, or the documentation ranges

    se-anonymise --manifest harness/scrub/x.json --hostnames a,b \\
                 --map ~/.local/state/se/scrub/x.json payload.json
    se-anonymise --check [--manifest …] [--hostnames a,b] payload.json

--check runs ONLY the detectors. The old check grepped with this tool's own
patterns and therefore could not fail; it is gone.

Two refusals guard the names channel, because a format-name field with no
names list no-opped while the tool printed `scrubbed`: a manifest carrying
format-name entries demands --hostnames up front, and any string leaf a
substituting class hands back unchanged is refused by path unless the KEEP
table names it as protocol/structural vocabulary — an unengaged substitution
is not a weaker scrub but no scrub at all.

The map is the REVERSE map — it de-anonymises every specimen it was used on —
and the secret is its twin. Both are refused anywhere inside ANY git working
tree (walked up to a .git), not merely inside this repository: the old guard
anchored on this repo's source layout and silently disarmed the moment the
tool was installed somewhere else.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import fnmatch
import hashlib
import base64
import hmac
import ipaddress
import json
import os
import re
import sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent))

from se_harness import detectors  # noqa: E402  (after the path insert above)

DISCLOSES = {"nothing", "identity", "location", "content", "secret"}
FORMATS = {"wwn", "guid_u64", "serial", "mac", "ipv4", "ipv6", "hostname",
           "name", "path", "prose", "unit_name", "object_path"}

# The formats whose whole VALUE is the identifier. Everything here is held to
# the survived-unchanged gate below: if the leaf is a WWN and scrubbing left it
# alone, no rule engaged and the run is lying when it prints `scrubbed`.
#
# `prose` is deliberately outside it, and it is the only format that is. A
# journal MESSAGE, a syslog line, a command line — these are unbounded text
# that MAY carry an identifier and routinely carries none, so "nothing changed"
# is the ordinary outcome rather than evidence of a hole. It still takes the
# same free-text pass every formatless entry takes, so a hostname or an address
# written inside prose is still substituted; what it exempts is only the
# refusal for having found nothing. Naming it as a format rather than inferring
# it from a missing one keeps the exemption a written, per-leaf claim a
# reviewer can disagree with — thirty formatless entries in the shipped
# manifests rely on that gate today, and they must go on relying on it.
PROSE = "prose"

# ── identifiers a producer writes in its own escaping ────────────────────
#
# The passes below read a leaf exactly as written, and systemd does not write
# an identifier only one way. ONE partition UUID appears three times in a
# single ListUnits row: plain in the device unit's description
# (/dev/disk/by-uuid/<uuid>), `\x2d`-escaped in the unit name systemd derives
# from that path, and escaped a second time as `_5cx2d` in the D-Bus object
# path derived from the name. Measured on a systemd 259 capture: 54 strings
# whose identifier appears only after folding, and ZERO findings from the
# independent detectors on the unscrubbed file — so neither the remover nor
# the checker could see two of the three spellings, and a `content`
# classification on those leaves would have claimed a substitution that never
# happened. That is worse than a refusal: it is a manifest saying the value
# was scrubbed.
#
# So a manifest may declare the ENCODING a leaf is written in, and the pass
# runs over the folded reading. The escapes are then restored BY POSITION,
# which is sound for exactly the reason rule 1 of DESIGN 21 exists: a
# substitution keeps its original's shape, so a UUID is still 8-4-4-4-12 and a
# WWN is still sixteen hex digits, and the character an escape stood for is
# still that character at the same offset. Where the pass changed the LENGTH —
# an address, a name from the names list — the restore is refused rather than
# guessed, because a re-escaped value of the wrong length is a unit name the
# host could not have produced.
#
# Both formats take prose's found-nothing exemption, and here it is sound
# rather than merely convenient: `ssh.service` folds to itself and carries
# nothing, while the case the gate exists for — a value that DOES carry an
# identifier and came back unchanged — can no longer arise from the escaping
# alone, because the pass has now read what the value encodes.
UNIT_ESCAPE = re.compile(r"\\x([0-9a-fA-F]{2})")
DBUS_PATH_ESCAPE = re.compile(r"_([0-9a-fA-F]{2})")

# Outermost encoding first: a D-Bus object path is the path-escaping OF an
# already-escaped unit name, so `_5cx2d` folds to `\x2d` and then to `-`.
ENCODED_FORMATS = {
    "unit_name": (UNIT_ESCAPE,),
    "object_path": (DBUS_PATH_ESCAPE, UNIT_ESCAPE),
}

# The formats for which "the pass found nothing" is an ordinary outcome rather
# than evidence of a hole. Held as a set so the gate below cannot learn one
# member and forget another — the shape that let a shipped manifest classify a
# field as scrubbed while nothing ran.
FOUND_NOTHING_EXEMPT = frozenset({PROSE, *ENCODED_FORMATS})

U64_MAX = 2**64 - 1

# Documentation ranges exist so examples need not borrow somebody's addresses.
DOC_V4 = "192.0.2"  # RFC 5737 TEST-NET-1
DOC_V6 = "2001:db8"  # RFC 3849

# Constants: protocol-assigned, identical everywhere, and load-bearing when a
# reader is trying to understand what a rule matches. Pinned by tests.
KEEP_V4 = {
    "0.0.0.0", "255.255.255.255",              # wildcard, broadcast
    "127.0.0.1", "127.0.0.0",                  # loopback
    "10.0.0.0", "172.16.0.0", "192.168.0.0",   # RFC 1918 network bases
    "100.64.0.0",                              # RFC 6598 CGNAT — the tailnet range
    "169.254.0.0", "169.254.169.254",          # link-local, cloud metadata
    "224.0.0.0", "224.0.0.1", "224.0.0.2",     # multicast
    "224.0.0.22", "224.0.0.251", "239.255.255.250",
}
KEEP_V6 = {
    "::", "::1",                               # unspecified, loopback
    "fe80::",                                  # link-local prefix
    "ff02::1", "ff02::2", "ff02::fb", "ff02::16", "ff05::c",
    "fd7a:115c:a1e0::",                        # Tailscale's published ULA prefix
}
# A MAC's group constants are the same on every LAN on earth: broadcast, IPv4
# and IPv6 multicast mappings, and the 802.1D bridge group. Substituting one
# turns a rule about a protocol into a rule about nothing — and a group
# address must never come back unicast, which is a change of meaning.
KEEP_MAC = {"ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff"}
KEEP_MAC_PREFIXES = ("01:00:5e", "33:33", "01:80:c2")

# The KEEP table for the survived-unchanged gate: the vocabulary a
# substituting field may legitimately hand back verbatim, because the token
# names a protocol, a daemon or a structure — never this machine. Everything
# else that a substituting class returns unchanged is a REFUSAL: a
# classification that changed nothing means no rule engaged, and the failure
# mode of shrugging is publication. Growing this table is a reviewed code
# change, which is the point.
KEEP_UNCHANGED_TOKENS = {
    # nftables' own words, seen beside addresses in the same member: ct
    # states, ICMP type names, family/protocol selectors.
    "established", "related", "new", "invalid", "untracked",
    "echo-request", "echo-reply", "destination-unreachable", "source-quench",
    "time-exceeded", "parameter-problem", "packet-too-big",
    "nd-redirect", "nd-neighbor-solicit", "nd-neighbor-advert",
    "nd-router-solicit", "nd-router-advert", "router-advertisement",
    "router-solicitation", "mld-listener-query", "mld-listener-report",
    "mld-listener-done",
    "ip", "ip6", "inet", "ipv4", "ipv6", "tcp", "udp", "icmp", "icmpv6",
    # Interface names software assigns identically on every install: the
    # loopback, and tailscaled's one device. They teach which daemon runs,
    # not which machine — an operator-named bridge is exactly not here.
    "lo", "lo*", "tailscale0", "tailscale0*",
    # zpool's group-vdev container words. They sit at the same position as a
    # vdev NAME — `zpool list -j -v` nests them inside `vdevs` — so the ~key
    # entry that classifies vdev names as identity classifies these too, and
    # a substituting class that hands back a word unchanged is a refusal by
    # design. These three are structure, spelled identically on every pool
    # on earth: the numbered forms (spare-3, log-0) were already covered by
    # the shape below, and the bare container words were not, because until
    # a pool with a spare was captured nothing had ever carried one.
    "spares", "logs", "l2cache",
}
KEEP_UNCHANGED_SHAPES = (
    # Bus topology: positional all the way down, identical on every machine
    # of the model (the phys_path lesson, DESIGN 21). Strictly numeric —
    # a serial's letters do not fit this grammar.
    re.compile(r"^pci-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}:[0-9a-fA-F]{2}:[0-9a-fA-F]{2}"
               r"\.[0-9a-fA-F](?:-[a-z]+(?:-?\d+)?)*$"),
    re.compile(r"^(?:scsi|sas|usb|ata)-\d+(?::\d+){1,3}$"),
    # zpool's structural vdev names: the software's own numbering, carried
    # under the same format-name entries as the operator-chosen pool name.
    re.compile(r"^(?:raidz[123]?|mirror|draid[123]?(?:[:\d]+[cds]?)*|spare|"
               r"replacing|indirect|log)-\d+$"),
    # @set-name references: the set's own name entry already ruled on it.
    re.compile(r"^@[\w-]+$"),
)

# The marker a substituted fingerprint carries so the independent checker can
# tell it from a real one — the same job 5cb0 does for 32-hex identifiers.
# Four base64 characters, which is exactly three bytes, so the rest of the
# body stays aligned and the token keeps OpenSSH's own length.
SSH_FINGERPRINT_MARKER = "ANON"
MD5_FINGERPRINT_MARKER = "A0A0"

# OpenSSH's own two renderings. Restated here rather than imported from the
# detectors, for the same reason every other shape is: the checker and the
# scrubber must be able to disagree, and a shared regex is a shared blind spot.
SSH_FINGERPRINT = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:SHA256:[A-Za-z0-9+/]{43}=?|MD5:(?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){15}[0-9a-f]{2})\b"
)

TAILNET_ULA = ipaddress.ip_network("fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48")
ULA = ipaddress.ip_network("fc00::/7")
CGNAT = ipaddress.ip_network("100.64.0.0/10")

# A PCI/SCSI/ATA topology token and an address are the same characters in the
# same order (0000:03:00.0, scsi-0:0:0:0). Shape cannot tell them apart —
# that is the whole lesson of DESIGN 21 — so the ADDRESS patterns anchor on
# the whole whitespace-delimited token and refuse any that carries a bus
# prefix. Whether such a field is scrubbed at all is the manifest's decision,
# never this exemption's.
EXEMPT_TOKEN_MARKS = ("pci-", "scsi-", "ata-", "usb-", "sas-", "nvme-")


# ── the secret, and where it must never live ─────────────────────────────


def _inside_git_tree(path: Path) -> bool:
    """True if path sits inside ANY git working tree, walked up to a .git.

    Not "inside this repository": the tool guards wherever it is installed,
    and the one place the reverse map will be put by accident is next to the
    thing it decodes — which is a checkout, wherever that checkout is.
    """
    resolved = path.resolve()
    return any((p / ".git").exists() for p in (resolved, *resolved.parents))


def _load_secret() -> bytes:
    env = os.environ.get("SE_SCRUB_SECRET")
    if env:
        return env.encode()
    state = Path(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or Path.home() / ".local" / "state")
    path = state / "se" / "scrub" / "secret"
    if _inside_git_tree(path):
        raise SystemExit(
            f"refusing secret at {path}: it sits inside a git working tree, "
            "one commit away from de-anonymising every specimen it keyed. "
            "Set SE_SCRUB_SECRET or move XDG_STATE_HOME out of the tree."
        )
    if not path.exists():
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        path.write_text(os.urandom(32).hex() + "\n")
        path.chmod(0o600)
    return path.read_text().strip().encode()


# ── keyed derivation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────


class Ctx:
    """One scrub run: the key, the reverse map, and the problems so far."""

    def __init__(self, secret: bytes, mapping: dict, hostnames: list[str],
                 entries: dict):
        self.secret = secret
        self.mapping = mapping  # "class:original" -> replacement, globally
        self.hostnames = sorted((h for h in hostnames if h), key=len, reverse=True)
        self.entries = entries
        self.problems: list[str] = []

    def derive(self, cls: str, value: str, extra: str = "") -> int:
        msg = f"{cls}:{value}" + (f"#{extra}" if extra else "")
        digest = hmac.new(self.secret, msg.encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()
        return int.from_bytes(digest[:16], "big")

    def remember(self, cls: str, original: str, make):
        key = f"{cls}:{original}"
        if key not in self.mapping:
            self.mapping[key] = make()
        return self.mapping[key]


# ── replacement builders, one per shape ──────────────────────────────────


def _hex16_replacement(ctx: Ctx, body: str) -> str:
    # NAA nibble 3 is "locally assigned": the one spelling that names no
    # vendor, mirroring the 02: locally-administered MAC. The detectors
    # recognise the nibble as the anonymised convention.
    n = ctx.derive("wwn", body.lower())
    width = len(body)
    return ctx.remember("wwn", body.lower(), lambda: "3" + f"{n:x}".zfill(width)[1:width])


def _hex32_replacement(ctx: Ctx, body: str) -> str:
    n = ctx.derive("machine-id", body.lower())
    return ctx.remember("machine-id", body.lower(), lambda: "5cb0" + f"{n % 16**28:028x}")


def _guid_replacement(ctx: Ctx, digits: str) -> str:
    """Same digit count, never above 2^64-1, and inside the 1844… band.

    The band is what lets a checker holding no key tell a replacement from a
    real GUID: 2^64-1 is 18446…, so the band reads as u64-max-adjacent, and a
    uniformly random real GUID lands in it with probability ~1e-4. Width is
    preserved for every width a u64 can carry — a 20-digit guid that came
    back 19 digits would silently stop exercising the parser.
    """
    width = len(digits)
    if width >= 15:
        unit = 10 ** (width - 4)
        lo, hi = 1844 * unit, min(1845 * unit - 1, U64_MAX)
    else:
        lo, hi = (10 ** (width - 1) if width > 1 else 0), 10**width - 1
    n = ctx.derive("guid", digits)
    return ctx.remember("guid", digits, lambda: str(lo + n % (hi - lo + 1)))


def _serial_replacement(ctx: Ctx, serial: str) -> str:
    n = ctx.derive("serial", serial)
    body = f"{n:026X}"
    keep = max(len(serial) - 4, 4)  # length preserved once past the marker
    return ctx.remember("serial", serial, lambda: "ANON" + body[:keep])


def _fingerprint_replacement(ctx: Ctx, fingerprint: str) -> str:
    """An OpenSSH key fingerprint, replaced by one of the same shape.

    Not a secret — it is a hash of a PUBLIC key — but it correlates: anyone
    holding the key recognises the estate it authenticates to, and the journal
    prints it on every accepted login. It has no shape the free-text pass knew
    and no name anybody would think to pass with --hostnames, so before this it
    survived both the scrubber and the independent detectors verbatim.

    The rendering is preserved rather than replaced with a marker, because the
    surrounding sshd line is the payload: `Accepted publickey ... ED25519
    SHA256:<44 chars>` has to go on reading like sshd wrote it, or the corpus
    stops being a specimen of the interface. Base64 of the keyed digest, cut to
    OpenSSH's own 43 characters, unpadded exactly as OpenSSH prints it.
    """
    algorithm, _, body = fingerprint.partition(":")
    n = ctx.derive("fingerprint", fingerprint)

    def build() -> str:
        # A SCRUBBED fingerprint announces itself, exactly as a scrubbed 32-hex
        # identifier announces itself with 5cb0. Without a marker the checker
        # cannot tell a substituted fingerprint from a real one and flags the
        # corpus forever — and the alternative, teaching it to recognise this
        # scrubber's arithmetic, is the shared blind spot the two halves of the
        # gate exist to avoid. ANON is this tool's own marker, already carried
        # by substituted serials.
        if algorithm.upper() == "MD5":
            octets = MD5_FINGERPRINT_MARKER + f"{n:032X}"[:32 - len(MD5_FINGERPRINT_MARKER)]
            return "MD5:" + ":".join(
                octets[i:i + 2].lower() for i in range(0, 32, 2))
        raw = base64.b64decode(SSH_FINGERPRINT_MARKER) + n.to_bytes(32, "big")[:29]
        return "SHA256:" + base64.b64encode(raw).decode().rstrip("=")[:43]

    return ctx.remember("fingerprint", fingerprint, build)


def _mac_replacement(ctx: Ctx, mac: str) -> str:
    low = mac.lower()
    if low in KEEP_MAC or low.startswith(KEEP_MAC_PREFIXES):
        return mac
    n = ctx.derive("mac", low)
    # 02: locally administered so the OUI names no vendor; a group address
    # (odd first octet) stays a group address — 03: is its local twin.
    first = 0x03 if int(low[:2], 16) & 1 else 0x02
    rest = [(n >> (8 * i)) & 0xFF for i in range(5)]
    return ctx.remember(
        "mac", low, lambda: ":".join(f"{o:02x}" for o in [first, *rest])
    )


def _host_replacement(ctx: Ctx, host: str) -> str:
    """Collision-free buckets: distinct originals -> distinct replacements.

    The old 100-bucket scheme collided by design (node1 and node2 shared a
    bucket), which merged two machines into one and taught a reader a join
    that does not exist. Collisions are retried away instead.
    """
    key = host.lower()
    cached = ctx.mapping.get(f"host:{key}")
    if cached is not None:
        return cached
    taken = {v for k, v in ctx.mapping.items() if k.startswith("host:")}
    attempt = 0
    while True:
        n = ctx.derive("host", key, extra=str(attempt) if attempt else "")
        candidate = f"host-{n % 16**6:06x}"
        if candidate not in taken:
            ctx.mapping[f"host:{key}"] = candidate
            return candidate
        attempt += 1


def _ip_replacement(ctx: Ctx, value: str) -> str | None:
    """An address, substituted WITHIN ITS OWN SPACE, or kept as a constant.

    Both rules were learned from committed corpus that got them wrong: a
    well-known constant is the same on every machine on earth and reveals
    nothing; everything else stays in its space because the space is part of
    the value's shape — a CGNAT address rewritten into 192.0.2.0/24 turns a
    rule about tailnet traffic into a rule about nothing. Spaces are decided
    by the ipaddress module, not by comparing literal base strings.

    Returns None when the value is not an address at all.
    """
    if value in KEEP_V4 or value.lower() in KEEP_V6:
        return value
    try:
        ip = ipaddress.ip_address(value)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if ip.is_multicast:
        return value  # a group is a constant, whatever the group
    cls = f"ipv{ip.version}"
    n = ctx.derive(cls, value.lower())

    def build() -> str:
        if ip.version == 4:
            if ip.is_loopback:
                return f"127.0.0.{n % 254 + 1}"
            if ip.is_link_local:
                return f"169.254.{n % 256}.{n % 254 + 1}"
            if ip in CGNAT:
                return f"100.{64 + n % 64}.{n // 64 % 256}.{n % 254 + 1}"
            if ip in ipaddress.ip_network("10.0.0.0/8"):
                return f"10.{n % 256}.{n // 256 % 256}.{n % 254 + 1}"
            if ip in ipaddress.ip_network("172.16.0.0/12"):
                return f"172.{16 + n % 16}.{n // 16 % 256}.{n % 254 + 1}"
            if ip in ipaddress.ip_network("192.168.0.0/16"):
                return f"192.168.{n % 256}.{n % 254 + 1}"
            return f"{DOC_V4}.{n % 254 + 1}"
        if ip in TAILNET_ULA:
            # a published prefix survives; only the host part identifies
            return f"fd7a:115c:a1e0::{n % 65536:x}"
        if ip.is_link_local:
            return f"fe80::{n % 65536:x}"
        if ip in ULA:
            return f"fd{n >> 32 & 0xFF:02x}:{n >> 16 & 0xFFFF:x}::{n % 65536:x}"
        return f"{DOC_V6}::{n % 65536:x}"

    return ctx.remember(cls, value.lower(), build)


def _cidr_replacement(ctx: Ctx, addr: str, length: int) -> str | None:
    """A CIDR is scrubbed as a unit: the base masked valid for its length.

    Substituting the address alone produced 100.89.226.124/23 in committed
    corpus — an address where a network base belongs, which is not a weaker
    specimen but a wrong one.
    """
    replaced = _ip_replacement(ctx, addr)
    if replaced is None:
        return None
    if replaced == addr:
        return addr  # a kept constant is already a valid base
    masked = str(ipaddress.ip_network((replaced, length), strict=False).network_address)
    ctx.mapping[f"cidr:{addr}/{length}"] = masked
    return masked


# ── the text pass, for strings that embed identifiers ────────────────────

WWN_SPELLED = re.compile(
    r"(wwn-0x|scsi-3|naa\.|nvme-eui\.|eui\.|0x)([0-9a-fA-F]{32}|[0-9a-fA-F]{16})"
)
BARE_HEX16 = re.compile(r"\b[0-9a-fA-F]{16}\b")
BYID_SERIAL = re.compile(r"\b((?:ata|scsi|sas|usb|nvme)-[\w.\-]+_)([A-Za-z0-9]{4,})\b")
UUID = re.compile(
    r"\b[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-"
    r"[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}\b"
)
HEX32 = re.compile(r"\b[0-9a-fA-F]{32}\b")
MAC = re.compile(r"\b(?:[0-9a-fA-F]{2}:){5}[0-9a-fA-F]{2}\b")
CIDR_V4 = re.compile(r"\b((?:\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3})/(\d{1,2})\b")
CIDR_V6 = re.compile(
    r"(?<![0-9a-fA-F:.])((?:[0-9a-fA-F]{0,4}:){2,7}[0-9a-fA-F]{0,4})/(\d{1,3})\b"
)
# Bounded so a match cannot start or end mid-token: the trailing guard keeps
# a following '.' out, the leading guard stops a compressed address being
# matched from its second half.
IPV6 = re.compile(
    r"(?<![0-9a-fA-F:.])(?:[0-9a-fA-F]{0,4}:){2,7}[0-9a-fA-F]{0,4}"
    r"(?![0-9a-fA-F:.])"
)
IPV4 = re.compile(r"\b(?:\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3}\b")
# Fifteen digits, not nineteen, and the number is `_guid_replacement`'s own:
# that builder treats >= 15 digits as the u64 band, so a floor of 19 here made
# the free-text pass blind to exactly the guids the builder was written to
# stand in for. A uniformly random u64 is BELOW 10^19 about half the time, and
# udev writes a ZFS pool guid into a by-uuid link in decimal — measured on a
# systemd 259 capture: `dev-disk-by\x2duuid-560891294865571655.device`, the
# pool guid DESIGN 21's opening example is about, eighteen digits, out
# verbatim. The independent detectors already floor at 16 under an id-ish key
# for the same reason; this is the removing side catching up.
GUID_TEXT = re.compile(r"\b\d{15,20}\b")


def _token_around(text: str, start: int, end: int) -> str:
    a = start
    while a > 0 and not text[a - 1].isspace():
        a -= 1
    b = end
    while b < len(text) and not text[b].isspace():
        b += 1
    return text[a:b]


def scrub_text(text: str, ctx: Ctx) -> str:
    """Substitute every identifying token exactly once.

    Each replacement is parked behind an opaque placeholder and restored at
    the end. Without that, later patterns eat earlier replacements — a MAC
    rewritten to 02:… is still colon-separated hex, so the IPv6 pattern
    would scrub the scrub. That happened; the shape is the one thing that
    must survive.
    """
    parked: list[str] = []

    def park(replacement: str) -> str:
        parked.append(replacement)
        return f"\x00{len(parked) - 1}\x00"

    # hostnames first, longest first; substring on purpose, no \b: the
    # boundary-anchored pattern walked straight past tank/<host>_backups
    # because '_' is a word character.
    for host in ctx.hostnames:
        pattern = re.compile(re.escape(host), re.IGNORECASE)
        if pattern.search(text):
            replacement = _host_replacement(ctx, host)
            text = pattern.sub(lambda _m, _r=replacement: park(_r), text)

    # Before every other pattern: a base64 body is 43 characters of mixed
    # alphanumerics, and the hex and serial patterns below will happily match
    # inside one and leave a fingerprint that is half-substituted and no longer
    # the shape sshd printed.
    text = SSH_FINGERPRINT.sub(
        lambda m: park(_fingerprint_replacement(ctx, m.group(0))), text)

    def spelled(match: re.Match) -> str:
        return park(match.group(1) + _hex16_replacement(ctx, match.group(2)))

    text = WWN_SPELLED.sub(spelled, text)

    def bare16(match: re.Match) -> str:
        if match.group(0).isdigit():
            return match.group(0)  # a 16-digit counter, not a hex identifier
        return park(_hex16_replacement(ctx, match.group(0)))

    text = BARE_HEX16.sub(bare16, text)
    text = BYID_SERIAL.sub(
        lambda m: m.group(1) + park(_serial_replacement(ctx, m.group(2))), text
    )

    def uuid(match: re.Match) -> str:
        h = _hex32_replacement(ctx, match.group(0).replace("-", ""))
        return park(f"{h[:8]}-{h[8:12]}-{h[12:16]}-{h[16:20]}-{h[20:32]}")

    text = UUID.sub(uuid, text)
    text = HEX32.sub(lambda m: park(_hex32_replacement(ctx, m.group(0))), text)
    text = MAC.sub(lambda m: park(_mac_replacement(ctx, m.group(0))), text)

    def cidr(match: re.Match) -> str:
        if _exempt(match):
            return match.group(0)
        masked = _cidr_replacement(ctx, match.group(1), int(match.group(2)))
        if masked is None:
            return match.group(0)
        return park(f"{masked}/{match.group(2)}")

    def _exempt(match: re.Match) -> bool:
        token = _token_around(match.string, *match.span())
        return any(mark in token for mark in EXEMPT_TOKEN_MARKS)

    text = CIDR_V4.sub(cidr, text)
    text = CIDR_V6.sub(cidr, text)

    def address(match: re.Match) -> str:
        original = match.group(0)
        if _exempt(match):
            return original
        replaced = _ip_replacement(ctx, original)
        if replaced is None or replaced == original:
            return original
        return park(replaced)

    text = IPV6.sub(address, text)
    text = IPV4.sub(address, text)
    text = GUID_TEXT.sub(lambda m: park(_guid_replacement(ctx, m.group(0))), text)

    return re.sub(r"\x00(\d+)\x00", lambda m: parked[int(m.group(1))], text)


def _fold_escapes(text: str, pattern: re.Pattern) -> tuple[str, list[tuple[int, str]]]:
    """(the decoded reading, where each escape stood in it).

    Each mark is the offset in the FOLDED text of the single character an
    escape stood for, beside the escape's own bytes — which is what lets the
    original spelling be put back once the pass has run.
    """
    parts: list[str] = []
    marks: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    index = length = 0
    for match in pattern.finditer(text):
        parts.append(text[index:match.start()])
        length += match.start() - index
        marks.append((length, match.group(0)))
        parts.append(chr(int(match.group(1), 16)))
        length += 1
        index = match.end()
    parts.append(text[index:])
    return "".join(parts), marks


def _restore_escapes(text: str, marks: list[tuple[int, str]]) -> str:
    """The escaped spelling again: every folded character back in its bytes."""
    parts: list[str] = []
    last = 0
    for offset, original in marks:
        parts.append(text[last:offset])
        parts.append(original)
        last = offset + 1
    parts.append(text[last:])
    return "".join(parts)


def _placed_marks(marks: list[tuple[int, str]], folded: str,
                  scrubbed: str) -> list[tuple[int, str]] | None:
    """Where each escape's character sits AFTER the pass, or None if unknowable.

    Same length is the ordinary case and needs no arithmetic: every identifier
    format keeps its original's shape (DESIGN 21 rule 1), so a UUID is still
    8-4-4-4-12 and every offset holds. A length CHANGE comes from the one
    mechanism that cannot preserve shape — an operator's word substituted from
    the names list — and then only the untouched prefix and suffix can be
    mapped: `dev-disk-by\\x2dlabel-labtank.device` has its escape in the
    prefix and survives, while an escape inside the region that moved is
    refused rather than re-escaped by guesswork, because a name put back in the
    wrong place is a name this producer could not have written.
    """
    if len(folded) == len(scrubbed):
        return marks
    prefix = 0
    while (prefix < min(len(folded), len(scrubbed))
           and folded[prefix] == scrubbed[prefix]):
        prefix += 1
    suffix = 0
    while (suffix < min(len(folded), len(scrubbed)) - prefix
           and folded[-1 - suffix] == scrubbed[-1 - suffix]):
        suffix += 1
    shift = len(scrubbed) - len(folded)
    placed = []
    for offset, original in marks:
        if offset < prefix:
            placed.append((offset, original))
        elif offset >= len(folded) - suffix:
            placed.append((offset + shift, original))
        else:
            return None
    return placed


def _scrub_encoded(value: str, fmt: str, path: tuple, ctx: Ctx) -> str:
    """A leaf written in a producer's own escaping, scrubbed through it."""
    folds, text = [], value
    for pattern in ENCODED_FORMATS[fmt]:
        text, marks = _fold_escapes(text, pattern)
        folds.append((text, marks))
    scrubbed = scrub_text(text, ctx)
    for folded, marks in reversed(folds):
        placed = _placed_marks(marks, folded, scrubbed)
        if placed is None:
            ctx.problems.append(
                f"{_dot(path)}: declared {fmt}, and a substitution moved the "
                "text an escape sat in, so the escaping cannot be put back "
                "where the producer wrote it — reclassify, or hand the run a "
                "names list that does not cut across an escaped span"
            )
            return value
        scrubbed = _restore_escapes(scrubbed, placed)
    return scrubbed


# ── the manifest, and the walk it governs ────────────────────────────────


def parse_pattern(key: str, stems: set[str]) -> tuple[str | None, tuple]:
    """(payload scope or None, the pattern's segments).

    Two escapes, both forced by the first tree-shaped collector whose payloads
    share a shape:

    A leading "<stem>:" scopes the entry, exactly as the "payload" member
    does, and exists because a JSON object holds a key ONCE — so a collector
    with four one-container-one-member payloads could classify that shape for
    only one of them. Recognised only when the prefix is a stem in this run,
    so a member that legitimately contains a colon still reads as pattern.

    A backslash-escaped dot is a literal dot inside one segment: a filename
    may carry one, and the tree seam keys payloads by filename.
    """
    scope = None
    head, colon, rest = key.partition(":")
    if colon and head in stems:
        scope, key = head, rest
    segments, current, escaped = [], "", False
    for char in key:
        if escaped:
            current += char
            escaped = False
        elif char == "\\":
            escaped = True
        elif char == ".":
            segments.append(current)
            current = ""
        else:
            current += char
    segments.append(current)
    return scope, tuple(segments)


def load_manifest(path: Path, stems: set[str] | None = None
                  ) -> tuple[list[tuple[tuple, dict]], dict[str, dict]]:
    """(path entries as (pattern, spec) pairs, whole-document entries).

    A LIST rather than a dict because two entries may carry one pattern when
    they are scoped to different payloads; entries_for narrows to a dict, and
    two surviving there is the ordinary two-entries refusal.
    """
    raw = json.loads(path.read_text())
    stems = stems or set()
    entries: list[tuple[tuple, dict]] = []
    seen: set[tuple] = set()
    wholes: dict[str, dict] = {}
    problems = []
    for key, spec in raw.items():
        if key.startswith("#"):
            continue  # commentary — JSON has no other way to carry it
        if isinstance(spec, dict) and "whole" in spec:
            # A whole-document entry classifies one text payload in one
            # stroke, so it must say WHICH — unscoped, it would claim every
            # document — and it carries no top-level classification, because
            # one entry serving both regimes would leave which one governs
            # to this file's reader.
            whole = spec["whole"]
            if not isinstance(whole, dict) or whole.get("discloses") not in DISCLOSES:
                problems.append(
                    f"{key}: whole.discloses must be one of {sorted(DISCLOSES)}"
                )
                continue
            if "format" in whole and whole["format"] not in FORMATS:
                problems.append(
                    f"{key}: whole.format must be one of {sorted(FORMATS)}"
                )
                continue
            if not (isinstance(spec.get("payload"), str) and spec["payload"]):
                problems.append(
                    f"{key}: a whole-document entry must name the payload it "
                    "classifies — unscoped, it would claim every document"
                )
                continue
            if "discloses" in spec or "format" in spec:
                problems.append(
                    f"{key}: whole and top-level classification on one entry "
                    "— exactly one regime must govern"
                )
                continue
            if spec["payload"] in wholes:
                problems.append(
                    f"{key}: payload {spec['payload']!r} already has a "
                    "whole-document entry — exactly one must"
                )
                continue
            wholes[spec["payload"]] = whole
            continue
        if not isinstance(spec, dict) or spec.get("discloses") not in DISCLOSES:
            problems.append(f"{key}: discloses must be one of {sorted(DISCLOSES)}")
            continue
        if "format" in spec and spec["format"] not in FORMATS:
            problems.append(f"{key}: format must be one of {sorted(FORMATS)}")
            continue
        if "payload" in spec and not (
            isinstance(spec["payload"], str) and spec["payload"]
        ):
            problems.append(
                f"{key}: payload must be the stem of the payload file this "
                "entry is scoped to, as a non-empty string"
            )
            continue
        scope, pattern = parse_pattern(key, stems)
        if scope is not None:
            if "payload" in spec:
                problems.append(
                    f"{key}: scoped by a prefix AND by a payload member — "
                    "exactly one must say which document this governs"
                )
                continue
            spec = dict(spec, payload=scope)
        identity = (spec.get("payload"), pattern)
        if identity in seen:
            problems.append(
                f"{key}: a second entry for this pattern under the same "
                "payload — one of them would silently govern and which is "
                "this file's iteration order"
            )
            continue
        seen.add(identity)
        entries.append((pattern, spec))
    if problems:
        raise SystemExit(f"{path}: not a usable manifest:\n  " + "\n  ".join(problems))
    return entries, wholes


def whole_for(wholes: dict[str, dict], payload: str) -> tuple[dict | None, list[str]]:
    """(the whole-document entry governing this payload, the patterns that
    matched it).

    A pattern is a stem or a glob over stems. Both are matched the same way —
    fnmatch on a stem with no wildcard is equality — so an exact entry keeps
    behaving exactly as it did. The match list is returned rather than
    resolved here because two matching entries is a refusal, not a choice:
    which one governs must never depend on this file's iteration order, and
    that is the same rule `_leaf_entry` applies to overlapping path patterns.
    """
    matched = [pattern for pattern in wholes if fnmatch.fnmatchcase(payload, pattern)]
    return (wholes[matched[0]] if len(matched) == 1 else None), matched


def entries_for(entries: list[tuple[tuple, dict]], payload: str) -> dict[tuple, dict]:
    """The entries that govern ONE payload document.

    Scoping is applied before any matching, so a `payload`-scoped entry is
    invisible to every other document — including to the ~key demand in
    _keys_are_data, which would otherwise read the devlinks map's root '*'
    entry as a claim that zpool's schema roots are data too.
    """
    return {
        pattern: spec
        for pattern, spec in entries
        if spec.get("payload") in (None, payload)
    }


def _matches(pattern: tuple, path: tuple) -> bool:
    return len(pattern) == len(path) and all(
        p == "*" or p == a for p, a in zip(pattern, path)
    )


def _prefix_matches(pattern: tuple, path: tuple) -> bool:
    return len(pattern) > len(path) and all(
        p == "*" or p == a for p, a in zip(pattern, path)
    )


def _dot(path: tuple) -> str:
    return ".".join(path) or "(root)"


def _leaf_entry(path: tuple, ctx: Ctx) -> dict | None:
    matches = [
        (pat, entry) for pat, entry in ctx.entries.items()
        if pat[-1] != "~key" and _matches(pat, path)
    ]
    if not matches:
        ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: no manifest entry classifies this leaf")
        return None
    if len(matches) > 1:
        ctx.problems.append(
            f"{_dot(path)}: matched by {len(matches)} entries "
            f"({', '.join('.'.join(p) for p, _ in matches)}) — exactly one must"
        )
        return None
    return matches[0][1]


def _key_entry(path: tuple, ctx: Ctx) -> dict | None:
    matches = [
        entry for pat, entry in ctx.entries.items()
        if pat[-1] == "~key" and _matches(pat[:-1], path)
    ]
    if len(matches) > 1:
        ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: more than one ~key entry applies")
        return None
    return matches[0] if matches else None


def _keys_are_data(path: tuple, ctx: Ctx) -> bool:
    """True when some entry walks through this dict's keys with a '*'.

    A '*' here means the keys are named things — pools, disks, datasets —
    rather than a schema's fixed members, and named things need a '~key'
    classification of their own: the name in the key is the same name that
    would be scrubbed in a value.
    """
    return any(
        pat[len(path)] == "*"
        for pat in ctx.entries
        if pat[-1] != "~key" and _prefix_matches(pat, path)
    )


def _substitute(value, fmt: str | None, path: tuple, ctx: Ctx):
    if value is None or isinstance(value, bool):
        return value  # nothing identifying survives in a bare state
    if isinstance(value, (int, float)):
        if fmt == "guid_u64" and isinstance(value, int):
            return int(_guid_replacement(ctx, str(value)))
        # A formatless entry is the free-text pass, and a bare number embeds
        # no token that pass could find — nft's match comparand holds ports
        # and fwmarks beside addresses in ONE member, so refusing its ints
        # would make the field unclassifiable at all. The pass-through stops
        # at 2^48, where real WWNs and GUIDs live (the detectors' own line):
        # an identifier-scale integer still refuses until its entry says
        # guid_u64, because that silence is the exact hole the old scrubber
        # shipped — zpool's integer GUIDs, out verbatim.
        if fmt is None and isinstance(value, int) and abs(value) < 2**48:
            return value
        ctx.problems.append(
            f"{_dot(path)}: a non-string leaf declared "
            "identity/location/content needs format guid_u64 — no other "
            "numeric identifier format exists yet"
        )
        return value
    if fmt == "guid_u64":
        if not value.isdigit():
            ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: declared guid_u64 but not digits")
            return value
        return _guid_replacement(ctx, value)
    if fmt == "wwn":
        replaced = WWN_SPELLED.sub(
            lambda m: m.group(1) + _hex16_replacement(ctx, m.group(2)), value
        )
        replaced = BARE_HEX16.sub(
            lambda m: m.group(0) if m.group(0).isdigit()
            else _hex16_replacement(ctx, m.group(0)),
            replaced,
        )
        if replaced == value:
            ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: declared wwn but no WWN found in it")
        return replaced
    if fmt == "serial":
        return _serial_replacement(ctx, value)
    if fmt == "mac":
        if not MAC.fullmatch(value):
            ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: declared mac but not a MAC")
            return value
        return _mac_replacement(ctx, value)
    if fmt in ("ipv4", "ipv6"):
        if "/" in value:
            addr, _, length = value.partition("/")
            masked = (
                _cidr_replacement(ctx, addr, int(length))
                if length.isdigit() else None
            )
            if masked is None:
                ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: declared {fmt} but not a CIDR")
                return value
            return f"{masked}/{length}"
        replaced = _ip_replacement(ctx, value)
        if replaced is None:
            ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: declared {fmt} but not an address")
            return value
        return replaced
    if fmt == "hostname":
        return _host_replacement(ctx, value)
    if fmt in ENCODED_FORMATS:
        return _scrub_encoded(value, fmt, path, ctx)
    # name, path, prose, or no format: free text that may embed anything.
    # All four take the SAME pass — prose differs only in that finding nothing
    # is not a refusal, because a log line is allowed to be about nothing.
    return scrub_text(value, ctx)


def _keeps_unchanged(value: str) -> bool:
    """True when a substituting class may hand this string back verbatim."""
    if not value:
        return True  # an empty string identifies nothing
    if value in KEEP_V4 or value.lower() in KEEP_V6:
        return True
    try:
        if ipaddress.ip_address(value).is_multicast:
            return True  # a group is a constant, whatever the group
    except ValueError:
        pass
    low = value.lower()
    if MAC.fullmatch(value) and (low in KEEP_MAC or low.startswith(KEEP_MAC_PREFIXES)):
        return True
    if low in KEEP_UNCHANGED_TOKENS:
        return True
    return any(shape.match(value) for shape in KEEP_UNCHANGED_SHAPES)


def _scrub_leaf(value, entry: dict, path: tuple, ctx: Ctx):
    discloses = entry["discloses"]
    if discloses == "nothing":
        return value
    if discloses == "secret":
        # never echo the value: this run's output may be the leak
        ctx.problems.append(
            f"{_dot(path)}: discloses=secret — a secret must never have been "
            "captured; scrubbing cannot repair this payload"
        )
        return value
    before = len(ctx.problems)
    replaced = _substitute(value, entry.get("format"), path, ctx)
    # Deny-by-default at the value layer: a substituting classification that
    # changed nothing means no rule engaged — the pool name that outlived a
    # run which printed `scrubbed` did exactly this. Strings only: a bare
    # number below 2^48 embeds no token by the pass-through's own argument,
    # and identifier-scale integers already refuse above. The value itself is
    # never echoed — a content leaf may BE the leak.
    if (
        isinstance(value, str)
        and replaced == value
        and len(ctx.problems) == before  # not already refused for this leaf
        # see FORMATS: prose and the two escaped formats may honestly hold none
        and entry.get("format") not in FOUND_NOTHING_EXEMPT
        and not _keeps_unchanged(value)
    ):
        ctx.problems.append(
            f"{_dot(path)}: classified {discloses} but the value survived "
            "scrubbing unchanged and is not a KEEP-table constant — no "
            "substitution rule engaged; reclassify it, extend KEEP, or hand "
            "the run the missing name via --hostnames"
        )
    return replaced


def _scrub_prefix_pair(node: dict, path: tuple, ctx: Ctx) -> dict:
    """{addr, len} is one value wearing two members: scrub it as a unit.

    Any other members the dict carries walk the manifest normally — only the
    address takes its length into account.
    """
    addr_entry = _leaf_entry((*path, "addr"), ctx)
    len_entry = _leaf_entry((*path, "len"), ctx)
    length = node["len"]
    if len_entry is not None:
        length = _scrub_leaf(node["len"], len_entry, (*path, "len"), ctx)
    addr = node["addr"]
    if addr_entry is not None and addr_entry["discloses"] not in ("nothing", "secret"):
        masked = _cidr_replacement(ctx, addr, node["len"])
        if masked is None:
            ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: addr/len pair but addr is not an address")
        elif masked == addr and not _keeps_unchanged(addr):
            # same deny-by-default as _scrub_leaf: this branch substitutes
            # outside it, so it carries the survived-unchanged gate itself
            ctx.problems.append(
                f"{_dot(path)}: classified {addr_entry['discloses']} but the "
                "prefix base survived scrubbing unchanged and is not a "
                "KEEP-table constant — no substitution rule engaged"
            )
        else:
            addr = masked
    elif addr_entry is not None:
        addr = _scrub_leaf(addr, addr_entry, (*path, "addr"), ctx)
    rest = {
        k: scrub_node(v, (*path, str(k)), ctx)
        for k, v in node.items() if k not in ("addr", "len")
    }
    return {**rest, "addr": addr, "len": length}


def scrub_node(node, path: tuple, ctx: Ctx):
    if isinstance(node, dict):
        if (
            isinstance(node.get("addr"), str)
            and isinstance(node.get("len"), int)
            and not isinstance(node.get("len"), bool)
        ):
            return _scrub_prefix_pair(node, path, ctx)
        key_entry = _key_entry(path, ctx)
        if key_entry is None and _keys_are_data(path, ctx):
            ctx.problems.append(
                f"{_dot(path)}: keys here are data (an entry uses '*' at this "
                f"position) but no '{_dot(path)}.~key' entry classifies them"
            )
        out = {}
        for k, v in node.items():
            new_key = k
            if key_entry is not None and isinstance(k, str):
                new_key = _scrub_leaf(k, key_entry, (*path, k), ctx)
            out[new_key] = scrub_node(v, (*path, str(k)), ctx)
        if len(out) != len(node):
            ctx.problems.append(f"{_dot(path)}: key substitution collided — refusing")
        return out
    if isinstance(node, list):
        return [scrub_node(v, (*path, str(i)), ctx) for i, v in enumerate(node)]
    entry = _leaf_entry(path, ctx)
    if entry is None:
        return node
    return _scrub_leaf(node, entry, path, ctx)


def scrub_whole(text: str, spec: dict, payload: str, ctx: Ctx) -> str:
    """One text document under one classification (a `whole` entry).

    Reuses _scrub_leaf so a whole document gets the same format handling,
    the same secret refusal and the same survived-unchanged gate as any
    leaf. The one difference is whitespace: a shaped format (hostname,
    mac, an address) substitutes a token, and the document carries its
    token with a trailing newline — so the token is cut out, substituted,
    and its surroundings are kept byte-for-byte, because a replay seam
    reads these files verbatim and a scrub must not also reformat.
    """
    entry = {"discloses": spec["discloses"]}
    fmt = spec.get("format")
    if fmt is not None:
        entry["format"] = fmt
    if fmt in (None, "name", "path"):
        # free text: the pass walks the whole document, whitespace and all
        return _scrub_leaf(text, entry, (payload,), ctx)
    head, core, tail = re.fullmatch(r"(\s*)(.*?)(\s*)", text, re.DOTALL).groups()
    if not core:
        ctx.problems.append(
            f"{payload}: classified {spec['discloses']} but the document is "
            "blank — nothing for the format to substitute"
        )
        return text
    return head + _scrub_leaf(core, entry, (payload,), ctx) + tail


# ── entry points ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


def _declares_addresses(entries: list[tuple[tuple, dict]],
                        wholes: dict[str, dict]) -> bool:
    # whole-document entries count too: a text payload classified as an
    # address is exactly as much of an address field as any JSON leaf
    return any(
        e.get("format") in detectors.ADDRESS_BEARING_FORMATS
        and e["discloses"] != "nothing"
        for e in (*(spec for _, spec in entries), *wholes.values())
    )


def _require_names(
    entries: list[tuple[tuple, dict]],
    wholes: dict[str, dict],
    hostnames: list[str],
) -> None:
    """A format-name field is operator-chosen words, and the ONLY mechanism
    that substitutes such words is the names list the run is handed. Without
    one, every name field no-ops while the tool prints `scrubbed` — the
    demonstrated leak. format hostname is exempt on purpose: there the leaf
    value IS the name and is substituted whole, no vocabulary needed.
    Whole-document entries answer to the same rule: a text payload classified
    format name no-ops without a names list exactly as a leaf does.
    """
    if hostnames:
        return
    named = [
        ".".join(pat) for pat, e in entries
        if e.get("format") == "name"
        and e["discloses"] in ("identity", "location", "content")
    ] + [
        payload for payload, e in wholes.items()
        if e.get("format") == "name"
        and e["discloses"] in ("identity", "location", "content")
    ]
    if named:
        raise SystemExit(
            "refusing to run without --hostnames: the manifest classifies "
            + ", ".join(sorted(named))
            + " as format name — operator-chosen words only the names list "
            "can substitute. Without it those fields no-op silently, and the "
            "failure mode of shrugging is publication."
        )


def run_check(args, hostnames: list[str]) -> None:
    """--check is the detectors, and ONLY the detectors.

    The scrubber must not verify itself: a checker that greps with the
    remover's own pattern cannot fail, and did not — 0 detections in 300
    fuzzed runs against a scrubber missing real WWNs.
    """
    from se_harness import detectors

    declares = False
    if args.manifest:
        entries, wholes = load_manifest(
            args.manifest, {path.stem for path in args.payloads})
        # the check is as blind to unnamed operator words as the scrub is:
        # a manifest that declares name fields demands a names source here too
        _require_names(entries, wholes, hostnames)
        declares = _declares_addresses(entries, wholes)
    dirty = False
    for path in args.payloads:
        if path.suffix == ".json":
            document: object = json.loads(path.read_text())
        else:
            # A text payload scans as {stem: text}: the stem is the one name
            # the document has, and it is what arms the detectors' id-ish
            # key context — a boot_id file holds a UUID that only reads as
            # an identifier because of what the file is called.
            document = {path.stem: path.read_text()}
        findings = detectors.scan(
            document,
            declares_address_fields=declares,
            hostnames=hostnames,
        )
        if findings:
            dirty = True
            print(f"{path}: {len(findings)} finding(s)")
            for f in findings:
                print(f"  {f.path} [{f.value_class}] {f.why}")
        else:
            print(f"{path}: no findings")
    if dirty:
        raise SystemExit(1)


def main() -> None:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("payloads", nargs="+", type=Path)
    parser.add_argument("--manifest", type=Path,
                        help="scrub manifest (harness/scrub/<collector>.json)")
    parser.add_argument("--hostnames", default="",
                        help="comma-separated real hostnames")
    parser.add_argument("--map", type=Path,
                        help="write/read the substitution (reverse) map")
    parser.add_argument("--check", action="store_true",
                        help="run only the independent detectors; scrub nothing")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    hostnames = [h.strip() for h in args.hostnames.split(",") if h.strip()]

    if args.check:
        run_check(args, hostnames)
        return

    if not args.manifest:
        raise SystemExit(
            "refusing to scrub without --manifest: this tool no longer "
            "guesses a value's class from its shape (DESIGN 21)"
        )
    if args.map and _inside_git_tree(args.map):
        raise SystemExit(
            f"refusing --map {args.map.resolve()}: the substitution map "
            "de-anonymises every specimen it was used on, and this path is "
            "inside a git working tree. Write it somewhere private."
        )

    entries, wholes = load_manifest(
        args.manifest, {path.stem for path in args.payloads})
    _require_names(entries, wholes, hostnames)
    mapping: dict[str, str] = {}
    if args.map and args.map.exists():
        mapping = json.loads(args.map.read_text())
    ctx = Ctx(_load_secret(), mapping, hostnames, entries)

    scrubbed: list[tuple[Path, object, bool]] = []
    for path in args.payloads:
        document: object = None
        if path.suffix == ".json":
            document = json.loads(path.read_text())
            if isinstance(document, (dict, list)):
                # The manifest view for THIS document, so a scoped entry
                # cannot reach a payload it was not written for. One Ctx
                # across all of them: the mapping is global by rule 2 of
                # DESIGN 21, and the same WWN in three payloads must come
                # back as one replacement.
                ctx.entries = entries_for(entries, path.stem)
                scrubbed.append((path, scrub_node(document, (), ctx), False))
                continue
        # No leaves for a dotted path to name — a text payload, whose native
        # format IS the payload (DESIGN 20), or a JSON payload that decodes to
        # one scalar. Classified whole or refused, the same deny-by-default as
        # an unclassified leaf, because the failure mode of passing it is
        # publication.
        spec, matched = whole_for(wholes, path.stem)
        if len(matched) > 1:
            ctx.problems.append(
                f"{path.stem}: matched by {len(matched)} whole-document "
                f"entries ({', '.join(sorted(matched))}) — exactly one must"
            )
            continue
        if spec is None:
            ctx.problems.append(
                f"{path.stem}: no whole-document manifest entry "
                "classifies this payload"
            )
            continue
        if path.suffix == ".json":
            # A scalar document is the leaf, so it takes _scrub_leaf directly:
            # scrub_whole's whitespace surgery is about a TEXT file carrying
            # its token with a trailing newline, and a JSON scalar carries no
            # surroundings to preserve.
            entry = {"discloses": spec["discloses"]}
            if spec.get("format") is not None:
                entry["format"] = spec["format"]
            scrubbed.append(
                (path, _scrub_leaf(document, entry, (path.stem,), ctx), False)
            )
            continue
        scrubbed.append(
            (path, scrub_whole(path.read_text(), spec, path.stem, ctx), True)
        )

    if ctx.problems:
        # nothing is written: a partial scrub looks exactly like a full one
        raise SystemExit(
            "refusing to write, the capture is not fully understood:\n  "
            + "\n  ".join(dict.fromkeys(ctx.problems))
        )

    for path, document, is_text in scrubbed:
        if is_text:
            # verbatim bytes but for the substitutions: the replay seam
            # serves this file as the native document, so no reformatting
            path.write_text(document)
        else:
            path.write_text(json.dumps(document, indent=2, sort_keys=False) + "\n")
        print(f"{path}: scrubbed")

    if args.map:
        args.map.write_text(json.dumps(ctx.mapping, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
        print(f"{args.map}: {len(ctx.mapping)} substitutions "
              f"(keep this OUT of any repository — it is the reverse map)")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
